I'm Callie reading four reviewer Kevin  Kiner Xebec Luigi's Mansion may directly  trace its lineage to the Super Mario  franchise but the series is in many ways  its complete antithesis  whereas Mario's adventures are filled  with platforming challenges to overcome  Luigi's have been decidedly more  methodical emphasizing careful  observation over quick reflex Luigi's  Mansion three very much continues this  tradition but the tightly crafted set  pieces assembled here illustrate just  how compelling the style of gameplay  remains and the new mechanics in freer  structure make it perhaps the best  installment in the series yet once again  Mario's cowardly brother finds himself  unwittingly thrust into the role of hero  when Mario Princess Peach and her  entourage of toads are kidnapped shortly  after the group checks into the last  resort hotel Luigi narrowly avoids this  same fate by escaping down a laundry  chute and landing in the hotel's  basement where he soon reunites with  professor egad and his trusty  poltergeist a modified vacuum cleaner  that can suck up ghosts the poltergeist  serves as the basis of Luigi's entire  range of actions in Luigi's Mansion 3  despite being a more add up jumper than  his brother Luigi's feet are planted  firmly to the floor throughout nearly  the entire adventure his primary means  of interacting with the environment  instead comes through the poltergeists  numerous abilities on top of being able  to suck up debris and blow out air the  vacuum comes equipped with both the  strobe and the dark light from Luigi's  Mansion dark moon complimenting these is  a handful of satisfying new abilities  the suction shot fires a plunger that  can touch to certain objects allowing  Luigi to swing or pull them while the  bursts release a gust of air that knocks  crowds of enemies back and lifts the  plumber momentarily off the ground the  most vital addition to Luigi's ghost  hunting repertoire however is the slam  latch onto a ghost and you'll charge up  a meter that lets you bash them  repeatedly on the floor inflicting extra  damage not only do these new abilities  feel like natural extensions of Luigi's  skill set they inject a welcomed bit of  action to the gameplay and open up new  approaches for taking on enemies you can  slam one ghost into any others that are  nearby damaging multiple foes at once  and later encounters will force you to  use the other skill  at your disposal as well making battles  consistently enjoyable outside of combat  the Poltergust most significant new  feature is the ability to summon a GUI  doppelganger of Luigi called GUI gee  this goopy clone retains the same  abilities as Luigi but his gelatinous  body can slip through fences grates and  even spike traps letting him bypass  seemingly insurmountable obstacles and  discover hidden corners of the hotel  certain enemies and objects will also be  much too large for Luigi to swing on his  own requiring GUI jeez extra set of  hands and a number of rooms feature  pressure-sensitive tiles that one  character will need to stand on while  the other acts independently once you  unlock GUI Guk through almost the entire  adventure cooperatively with another  nearby player and the game lends itself  well to either co-op or solo play many  of the puzzles you'll encounter require  Luigi and GUI G to work in tandem which  makes exploring the hotel with a friend  enjoyable solo players meanwhile can  swap between Luigi and Luigi to quickly  take control of either character as the  situation demands  you'll never encounter a scenario that  can't be overcome solo although a  handful of bosses and puzzles are  clearly designed with a second player in  mind and are a bit more cumbersome when  you're juggling both characters much  like dark moon Luigi's Mansion 3 makes  clever use of your small clash of  abilities every puzzle you encounter  while exploring the last resort can be  surmounted by observing your  surroundings and employing some  combination of these skills although it  certainly won't seem that way for many  you'll frequently come across puzzles  that offer no obvious solution which  makes finally figuring out the answer  all the more satisfying on top of that  the hotel houses a wealth of  collectibles to find coins pearls bars  of gold and other valuables are tucked  away in treasure chests toilets in any  other compartment you can imagine  encouraging you to poke around there are  also six unique gems to find on each  floor many of these are deviously hidden  and you'll need to study your  surroundings carefully to figure out how  to uncover them which makes taking the  time to explore every nook and cranny of  the building constantly rewarding each  floor of the last resort is its own  self-contained level and adheres to a  different theme running the gamut from  restaurants and gift shops to medieval  castles and ancient Egyptian pyramids  this variety keeps the adventure  pleasantly surprising throughout gaining  access to a new floor is always a  delight because you never quite know  what to expect  the elevator door opens in order to  reach these floors you'll first need to  take on a boss ghost and retrieve the  elevator button in their possession  these boss encounters are another  highlight each one has a distinct  personality that's charmingly conveyed  through their animations these  personalities help elevate the boss's  above the rather forgettable ones from  dark moon and each battle makes that of  Luigi's skill set in clever ways the  controls however will occasionally get  in your way to shine the dark light you  need to hold the X button which means  you can't move the right analog stick to  adjust your aim while using that ability  the game compensates for this by letting  you aim using motion controls but you're  limited to aiming up and down making an  inelegant solution the suction shot  suffers from a similar issue rounding  out the package are two dedicated  multiplayer modes scare scraper and  scream park the former challenge is up  to eight players either locally or  online to complete successive floors of  a tower within a strict time limit  scream park meanwhile is a local only  party mode in which two teams compete  against one another in one of three  different mini-games both modes can be  fun diversions particularly scare  scraper which straddles the line between  competitive and cooperative however  they're comparatively shallow and lack  the same appeal as the main game while  the multiplayer modes might not hold  your attention for long the strength of  the Luigi's Mansion series has always  stem from the satisfaction of exploring  its carefully constructed settings and  in that regard Luigi's Mansion 3  certainly succeeds this game may not  radically diverge from the series  formula but it offers up another  meticulously crafted set of challenges  to overcome while smoothing out some of  the issues that held dark moon back and  the sense of accomplishment you feel  when you clear a particularly  head-scratching obstacle is just as  potent now as the first time Luigi  unwillingly stepped into a Haunted  Mansion  [Music]
 

